Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 21:11:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 38/52] kernel: prepare the sync object wait protocol (#4280)

* ci: run the latest VitaAutotests

* kernel: hand out each sync object's resource in one place

The semaphore, mutex, rwlock, event flag and simple event each move the code that gives waiters
what they wait for into a private serve(), which their signal, unlock and set calls use.
No behavior change.

* kernel: run a wait's callbacks from the wait loop

ThreadState::wait ran notified callbacks itself before returning. It now returns NOTIFY_CALLBACK,
and each wait loop runs them with run_callbacks() at the same point, so the loop decides what the
waiter does around them. No behavior change.

* kernel: let a waiter that leaves give its turn to the waiters behind it

A wait that ended by its timeout or its thread's end took nothing, but the waiters behind it
weren't served what they could take then. Each sync object now waits through wait_on(), which
then asks the object to serve() its other waiters, as a signal does. serve() becomes a sync object
virtual that does nothing by default, for the condition variable, the timer and the message pipe,
which keeps its own loop for now.

* kernel: write a sync object wait's time left back in wait_on

Every sync object wait computed its deadline from the guest timeout before wait_on() and wrote
the time left back after it. wait_on() now takes the guest timeout and does both, as the wait
protocol's own step. No behavior change.

* kernel: note two Vita behaviors the sync objects don't copy

A send that hands bytes to a waiting DONT_REMOVE receive leaves a Vita's message pipe out of step,
and a lightweight mutex deleted before a signaled condition waiter gets back to it leaves that
waiter blocked. Both stay out on purpose.

(cherry picked from commit ed0b71271fa9632c2d81532ef87da0cddf2bc970)
